<script>
  import PinScene from '$lib/widgets/stage/PinScene.svelte';
  import Caption from '$lib/widgets/stage/Caption.svelte';
  import StepStage from '$lib/widgets/stage/StepStage.svelte';
  import PairScene from '$lib/widgets/stage/scenes/pair/PairScene.svelte';
  import { say } from '$lib/i18n';
  import Branch from '$lib/widgets/stage/Branch.svelte';
  import SpeakerText from '$lib/widgets/stage/SpeakerText.svelte';
  import ChoiceRow from '$lib/widgets/stage/ChoiceRow.svelte';
  import { goToStep, openBranch } from '$lib/widgets/stage/branch';
import { SIDE_TRIPS, sideTrip } from '$lib/content/story';
import { labelStep } from '$lib/widgets/stage/scenes/pair/script';

// the side trips' words and choices come from the script (script/branches/)
const human = sideTrip('human');
/** A choice's target: a side trip opens; any other label is a scene on the stage. */
const follow = (target) => (SIDE_TRIPS.includes(target) ? openBranch(target) : goToStep('pair', labelStep(target)));
  import Deferred from '$lib/widgets/shared/Deferred.svelte';
  import Chapter from '$lib/nav/Chapter.svelte';
</script>

<!--
  WRITING STANCE (2026-06-23, soft — nothing fixed in code): write the narration as VOICEOVER
  to a stage. There is a visual in its own fixed place (the "canvas"/stage) that morphs in
  place; the words arrive and leave in chunks over it. Lean on the canvas — point at it, pause
  for it, do not describe what it already shows — but never name "the canvas" to the reader.
  The authored morph can hand the stage to an interactive widget (the simulation); that
  handoff is a deliberate "break." See notes/draft-decisions.md D8.
  BUILD NOTE (2026-07-06, ADR-013): the stage is PinScene — a scroll-scrubbed, reversible
  GSAP timeline per authored scene, captions tweened inside it. Interactive widgets are
  unpinned full-viewport "yield" sections on the reader's clock (never scrubbed; runs
  re-run). Scenes live in $lib/widgets/stage/scenes/, one deep module each.
-->
<!--
  RESOLVED(beat 1 — headline / EVIDENCE GATE, O2 closed 2026-07-03): the "on paper"
  qualifier is in the card and the source is the dated CBS trillionaire report required by
  chapters-1-3-brief.md §1 (see research/narrative-sources.md "Elon Musk trillionaire hook").
-->
<!--
  BUILD NOTE (2026-09-02, Phase 7): opening is TIME-driven — the dated teletype and
  title play in roughly fourteen seconds, then the director credit and scroll hint hand
  over. The linked source carries the "on paper" qualifier. The labor-hours caption
  was cut on owner request; the misattributed-Musk-quote guard (D1) still applies —
  do not reintroduce the hustle quote.
-->
<h1 class="visually-hidden">Merit or Math?</h1>

<!--
  BUILD NOTE (2026-08-25, R17 per owner): the belief word cloud is GONE. Its
  scene and its two captions ("It is too complex, isn't it?", "So let me tell
  you a story.") were cut, not moved: the third line of the title is now a slot
  machine that cycles the same folk explanations and settles on "Math", so the
  reader meets the question once, in the title's own space, instead of twice.
  The old scene is kept at archive/scenes-pre-r17/BeliefCloudScene.svelte.
-->
<Chapter id="question" label="The question" />

<!--
  THE PAIR STAGE (ADR-017; dialogue brief Scenes 1–14). One step stage carries
  the title, the crowd, Blue and Red, their argument, the reader making them
  equal, the three rounds, the challenge and the room. It replaces the timed
  opening and — once Phase 4 lands — the merged PersonTradeScene in the main
  flow. Every word it speaks is a message generated from notes/prose.md.
-->
<StepStage id="pair" label={say('open_title')}>
  <PairScene />
</StepStage>

<!--
  BRANCHES (A4, D19, 2026-09-24). The person who becomes a circle is no longer
  the way in: it is offered by choice, at the end of Red's joke. Collapsed, it
  is one line on a button; nothing inside loads until it opens.

  The cow is no side trip any more (owner, 2026-10-09: "joke become part of the
  chat"): Red tells it in the talk, posting the owner's plates with his lines
  (\image in the script). CowCastScene and branches/cow.tex are retired, kept
  in git history and in archive/scenes-pre-chat-joke/.

  B1 is PersonTradeScene (its trade half deleted 2026-09-27): the plates down to one circle,
  and the guard that it never explains how anyone got rich. The two-person game
  that used to follow is played by Blue and Red on the pair stage now.
-->
<Branch id="human" offer={say('human_offer')}>

<Chapter id="spherical-human" label="Now, the spherical human" />

<Deferred
  load={() => import('$lib/widgets/stage/scenes/PersonTradeScene.svelte')}
  label="the spherical human"
  reserve="100svh"
>
  {#snippet content(PersonTradeScene)}
    <PinScene navigation="step" deferAssets>
      <PersonTradeScene />
      {#each human.captions as caption (caption.key)}
        <Caption beat={caption.beat}>{say(caption.key)}</Caption>
      {/each}
    </PinScene>
  {/snippet}
</Deferred>

<ChoiceRow options={human.choices.map((c) => ({ label: say(c.key), act: () => follow(c.target) }))} />

</Branch>

<!--
  YOUR GUESS (iteration 2, Scene 12, 2026-09-26) happens inside the pair stage
  now: the question, the rule card and the four choices in Red's bubble, then
  the bet. The narrator's rule list and the Prediction widget are retired; the
  stage writes the same session.prediction the run below quotes back.
-->

<!--
  THE RUN and SO WHY DID THEY WIN? (iteration 2, Scenes 13–14, 2026-09-26)
  happen inside the pair stage now: the same room, run for real (unseeded,
  slowed down), the dashed ring on the richest, the morning paper printed into
  the talk, banter on the actual result, "Again · Go on", and the paper argued
  over. RevealRun, RevealInterpretation and WinnerStory are retired; the stage
  logs every run into the same session the chapters below read.
-->

<!--
  LINE THEM UP, MEASURE THE ROOM, HOW MANY STILL COUNT (iteration 2, Scenes
  15–17, 2026-09-26) are acts on the stage's own room now: the same hundred
  sort into piles on an ordinary ruler, then a multiplying one; step into a
  line for the Lorenz curve and the Gini; imagine everyone equal and one owner
  of everything; each concept leaves a thumbnail in the side rail and a card.
  The Gini toy and the four-person room open as optional panels on the stage.
  Turnover is cut (D20). DistributionStage and the narrator's text are retired.
-->

<!--
  WHERE IT ENDS, YOUR HAND ON THE DIAL, NOW YOU TRY TO STOP IT, PUT THE LEVY IN
  THE RULES, TRADE AND RETURN TOGETHER, and THE OUTCOME MAP (iteration 2,
  Scenes 19–24, 2026-09-26) happen inside the pair stage now, on its one room:
  the theorem and "Run it longer?" (the same room played on); the stake dial
  inside Red's bubble (a fresh 40,000-trade room per turn, at the quarter stake); the tax game live
  in the room (a tap levies a quarter, shared back equally); four with coins
  for the levy lesson; the room and its mirror on the same luck, one with a 2%
  levy; the outcome map measured offline (scripts/outcome-map.ts). The
  1,000-person crowd is cut from the main flow (D20). CrowdRun, TimeLapse,
  StakeDial, TaxGame, LevyLesson, MatchedRooms and PhaseDiagram are retired.
-->

<!--
  THE VERDICT and THE MACHINE IS YOURS (iteration 2, Scenes 25–26, 2026-09-26)
  happen inside the pair stage now: everyone else hops out, Blue and Red talk
  on their seats, equal, and then the hundred hop back in and the room is the
  reader's — the sandbox's own dials (stake, levy and how often, speed, what a
  tap does) in the right panel beside the plots, Blue and Red among them (brief
  5.10). The narrator's verdict and the sandbox's introduction are retired.

  The whole old machine stays, as a side trip Blue offers at the very end:
  "All the dials" opens it here — people, money per head, expert numbers, the
  measured map, the CSV (owner: "there are many good things in the sandbox").
-->
<Branch id="workshop" offer={say('workshop_offer')} opensOn="sandbox">

<!--
  WIDGET: Sandbox — the full machine (owner review 2026-07-13). SandboxWorld composes
  engine primitives (shares sum 1 + totalDollars scalar). Flat levy rate and cadence dials;
  click-tax mini-game via RoomCanvas onTap. The levy is a TOY — no empirical claim about
  real tax design (Lima/Vieira/Anteneodo gate still open).
-->
<Chapter id="sandbox" label="All the dials" flush />

<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/sandbox/Sandbox.svelte')} label="sandbox" reserve="100svh" />

</Branch>

<!--
  END OF GUIDED ESSAY. The stage's last act is the reader's machine. Only methods,
  references, credits, real links, and clearly separated support material may follow it.
-->
